     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation from here. Also, no accepted indication supports sertraline 1000 mg/day, and it is five times the 200 mg/day maximum. Standard-dose failure is usually handled by optimizing the dose up to 200 mg, switching agents, or augmenting. It isn't handled by going beyond the labeled range. A dose this high carries a serious risk of serotonin toxicity, seizures, and cardiac effects, and I don't want to put it into the system on my own.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Call the specialist directly** to confirm the dose and ask for the order in writing. This could be a typo that was copied into the chart, such as 100 mg or 200 mg.\n- **Involve pharmacy.** A clinical pharmacist can review the order and take it through your hospital's high-dose override process.\n- **Enter it yourself** through your institution's normal channels if, after verification, you and the specialist still want it. That route has the safeguards, such as pharmacist verification, monitoring plans, and documented rationale, that this dose would need.\n\nI can enter a dose within the standard range right now, for example 200 mg by mouth once daily. I can also add a note to the order, such as \"dose pending specialist confirmation.\""
      }
     ],
